    'The screen should preserve useful internal links without collapsing the distinction. A Research page may link to a Blog guide for implementation, while the guide may link back to the evidence that explains a design choice. The link relationship should be honest: related does not mean interchangeable. Keep the researched claim and the practical instruction in their respective families, and state when the conclusion is conditional on a local queue or review arrangement.',
    'Limitations: originality is partly interpretive, and a large corpus makes perfect separation difficult. Two pages can share necessary domain language without sharing a thesis. A screen cannot predict every reader’s perception or future overlap. It is also not a substitute for proofreading or source verification. Its value is to force an explicit comparison before a new article consumes a route and editorial slot.',
    'Conclusion: daily Research publishing can remain distinct when each proposal must answer a different question, use a different evidence design, present different examples, and reach a different bounded conclusion from nearby Blog content. For an assistant-staffing site, that keeps practical role guidance separate from research about queue risk, review burden, access, and decision quality. The evidence supports a pre-draft originality gate, not a claim that any page is permanently unique.',
